It’s now been over a month since I left home and set out to begin serving God in a different field. It has been a long, tiring endeavor, but I am now starting to feel my feet beneath me and am pressing on to the work I’ve come to do. Let me fill you in on what has happened thus far:

Weeks 1-2 (August 20-31st): Global Orientation (or GO) Conference (in Delden Netherlands)

Weeks 3-5 (September 1-21): Tool Kit (In Halesowen U.K.)

Week 6 (September 22-today): Normal routine begins

GO Conference:

I’ve told most of you a little bit about this, but it is a lot easier to explain after you’ve been to it. GO is a time of preparation for all OM missionaries who are going to a new field. Even if you are a veteran of OM, you must go to GO to get reversed in being ready for the new culture. We are prepared technically and mental through exercises and training sessions on how to deal with culture shock and being sensitive to the culture we are entering, as well as to the culture we are leaving. But we also spend a great deal of time at GO preparing spiritually for the work ahead. I found it to be a great experience, and when I have a few more things sorted out here I’ll try to send some pictures.

Tool Kit:

This is the training specific to OM-Lifehope (OM-Lh), the field of OM where I serve. We had three weeks of training in various forms of evangelism – from drama and puppets to balloon animals and face painting. If you can think of it, OM-Lh probably has it. We had an introduction to many of these and more during those three weeks. As we train, individual strengths and interests are identified. It was a little different for me because I had a skill set that they already had planned for me to use. This made me classified as a GS, in contrast to the GA. The meaning of these letters is a long-winded explanation, so I’ll spare you that now. If you are interested email me back and I’ll explain in greater detail.

One great experience I want to share was the day we went out and did evangelism in the city centre here in Halesowen. My partner, Megan, and I kept connecting with people who were either too busy or already Christians (the latter one being a great problem to have! Praise Jesus for those who are already saved!). Finally I got bold and decided to stop three school boys. Nervous way more than I showed, I started talking to them and asked them a few questions.

One question kind of sets the stage for the whole conversation: I asked “Do you think you are a good person?” Boy on the left tentatively, “I think so.” Boy in the middle, “Probably, not sure.” Boy on the right with zero hesitation and laughing, “Oh no, I’m the rotten one in the bunch.” Later it came out that they were a Christian, used to go to church, and never went to church respectively. The boy on the left brushed everything I said aside as if it was nothing, but found himself quickly alone in that as the middle boy was really struck by the words God gave me. We were taught that someone is ready for the gospel when they have been left speechless by what God has done for them.I left each of the three boys with a million pound bill. They laughed and took them. On the back of these bills was the gospel message for them to read later. I hope and pray that the middle boy finds his way “Home.” I can only hope that the seeds planted landed in soil ready to grow.

Megan filled me in later on one key piece I missed. The boy on the left, who said he was a Christian, was quiet so I barely noticed him. But Megan said he slowly grew in confidence and excitement the whole time. Pray that this experience will lead him to more openly talk to his friends about his faith.

As a funny piece at the end of this conversation: I had been trying to speak in an English accent the whole time. The boy on the left as he was walking away asked “Can I ask were you’re from? I can’t quite place your accent.” Dropping into my normal accent I respond, “I’m trying not sound completely like an American.” Without a moment’s hesitation he responded “You’re failing.” xD

